PREAMBLE
============================================================================

This Work (defined in Section 1) is created and stewarded by ᓂᐲᔥ
Nbiish-Justin Kenwabikise ᑭᓇᐙᐱᑭᓯ (the "Licensor"), a member of the Grand
Traverse Band of Ottawa and Chippewa Indians ("GTB"), a federally recognized
sovereign tribal nation, and a descendant of the Beaver Island Band of Ottawa
and Chippewa ("Beaver Island Band" or "BIB"), whose families are today
enrolled across both the Grand Traverse Band and the Little Traverse Bay Bands
of Odawa Indians ("LTBB"). The Beaver Island Band never ceded its inherent
relationship to the Beaver Island archipelago, its waters, or its cultural
patrimony through any act of Congress or voluntary surrender of rights
recognized by the Band itself.

This License is grounded in the inherent sovereignty of Indigenous peoples to
protect, control, and govern the use of their intellectual and cultural
property — a sovereignty that predates the United States Constitution and was
never surrendered. It expressly integrates and asserts rights under:

    (a)  United States copyright law (17 U.S.C.), which supplies this
         License's primary enforcement machinery, including the doctrine that
         license CONDITIONS are enforceable as limits on the scope of the
         copyright permissions granted (Jacobsen v. Katzer, 535 F.3d 1373
         (Fed. Cir. 2008));
    (b)  Federal Indian law, including the reserved rights doctrine, the
         Indian canons of construction, treaty rights under the Treaty of
         Washington (1836), 7 Stat. 491, and the Treaty of Detroit (1855),
         11 Stat. 621, and the tribal-court consent doctrine of Montana v.
         United States, 450 U.S. 544, 565 (1981);
    (c)  Traditional Knowledge (TK) Labels and protocols developed through
         the Local Contexts initiative;
    (d)  Indigenous Data Sovereignty frameworks: the CARE Principles for
         Indigenous Data Governance (Carroll et al., Data Science Journal
         19:43 (2020), DOI 10.5334/dsj-2020-043) and IEEE 2890-2025 (IEEE
         Recommended Practice for Provenance of Indigenous Peoples' Data);
    (e)  International Indigenous-rights instruments, including the United
         Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples (UNDRIP,
         2007) and the American Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous
         Peoples (OAS, 2016), each according to its true legal status as
         stated in Annex A; and
    (f)  The laws, customs, and traditions of the Grand Traverse Band of
         Ottawa and Chippewa Indians and of the Beaver Island Band families.

Nothing in this License claims for the Licensor, an individual, the sovereign
immunity that belongs only to tribal governments (see Section 16). Nothing in
this License takes any position in any dispute between the Grand Traverse Band
and the Little Traverse Bay Bands concerning the Beaver Island archipelago;
this License protects the Work and the cultural patrimony of the Beaver Island
archipelago and its descendant families, wherever enrolled.

