1. DEFINITIONS AND TRADITIONAL KNOWLEDGE TAXONOMY
============================================================================

For purposes of this License:

1.1  "Work" means the software, source code, object code, documentation,
     data structures, datasets, user interfaces, designs, text, audio,
     video, language resources, and any other original expression authored
     or stewarded by the Licensor and distributed under this License,
     together with all associated Traditional Knowledge and Cultural
     Expressions, whether or not separately copyrightable.

1.2  "Traditional Knowledge" (TK) means knowledge, know-how, skills,
     innovations, practices, and learning systems developed, sustained, and
     transmitted within the Anishinaabe (Ottawa/Chippewa/Odawa) communities
     described in the Preamble, including ecological, fisheries, navigational,
     linguistic, medicinal, and spiritual knowledge embodied in or informing
     the Work. This definition follows the usage of the WIPO Intergovernmental
     Committee on Intellectual Property and Genetic Resources, Traditional
     Knowledge and Folklore (IGC).

1.3  "Traditional Cultural Expression" (TCE) means any form in which
     Traditional Knowledge and culture are expressed, including music, dance,
     design, oral literature, ceremony, nomenclature, and visual art,
     consistent with WIPO IGC usage.

1.4  "Associated Traditional Knowledge" has the meaning given in the WIPO
     Treaty on Intellectual Property, Genetic Resources and Associated
     Traditional Knowledge (adopted May 24, 2024): Traditional Knowledge
     associated with genetic resources used or claimed in an invention.

1.5  "Cultural Patrimony" has the meaning given in the Native American
     Graves Protection and Repatriation Act (NAGPRA), 25 U.S.C. § 3001(3):
     objects having ongoing historical, traditional, or cultural importance
     central to the community itself, rather than property owned by an
     individual, which cannot therefore be alienated, appropriated, or
     conveyed by any individual.

1.6  "Derivative Data" means any data, dataset, corpus, embedding, feature
     representation, statistical model, annotation set, or index produced in
     whole or in part from the Work or from output of the Work.

1.7  "Model" means any machine-learning model, neural network, large
     language model, classifier, or similar computational system, including
     its weights and parameters, trained or fine-tuned in whole or in part
     on the Work or on Derivative Data.

1.8  "Community" means, collectively: (i) the Grand Traverse Band of Ottawa
     and Chippewa Indians and its members; (ii) the families of the Beaver
     Island Band of Ottawa and Chippewa and their descendants, whether
     enrolled as members of the Grand Traverse Band, of the Little Traverse
     Bay Bands of Odawa Indians, or of any other federally recognized tribe,
     and regardless of federal acknowledgment status of the Beaver Island
     Band itself; and (iii) the Little Traverse Bay Bands of Odawa Indians
     and its members, but solely with respect to Community Benefit uses
     described in Section 11 and solely to the extent such use does not
     facilitate the alienation of Cultural Patrimony.

1.9  "Community Protocols" means the laws, customs, and traditions
     governing access to, use of, and care for Traditional Knowledge,
     Cultural Expressions, and Cultural Patrimony, including protocols of the
     GTB and the protocols of Beaver Island Band families, as set out or
     incorporated in Annex B.

1.10 "Consensual Relationship" means the consensual relationship between
     the Licensor (a tribal member) and each Licensee formed by the
     Licensee's use of the Work under this License, being a "consensual
     relationship... through commercial dealing, contracts, leases, or other
     arrangements" within the meaning of Montana v. United States, 450 U.S.
     544, 565 (1981).

1.11 "Treaty-Protected Interests" means the rights reserved by the Ottawa
     and Chippewa nations in the Treaty of Washington (1836), 7 Stat. 491 —
     including the usufructuary rights of hunting, fishing, and gathering and
     the "usual privileges of occupancy" reserved in its Article Thirteenth —
     and in the Treaty of Detroit (1855), 11 Stat. 621, together with
     aboriginal and inherent rights never ceded.

1.12 "Licensee" means any individual, corporation, partnership, limited
     liability company, government agency, university, research institution,
     artificial-intelligence system operator, or other entity that uses,
     reproduces, modifies, distributes, or prepares derivative works of the
     Work, in whole or in part, in any medium.

