4. TRADITIONAL KNOWLEDGE (TK) PROTECTION AND LABELING
============================================================================

4.1  ASSERTION OF DUAL RIGHTS. The Work embodies Traditional Knowledge and
     Cultural Expressions. The Licensor asserts both western intellectual
     property rights and Indigenous rights over the Work, and nothing in this
     License waives, exhausts, or diminishes the latter.

4.2  TK LABELS. This Work carries the following Traditional Knowledge
     Labels, following the Local Contexts taxonomy (Provenance, Protocol, and
     Permission Labels; see localcontexts.org):

     (a)  TK Attribution (TK A) — community-specific rules govern how
          attribution must be given to the creator, the GTB, and the Beaver
          Island Band families;
     (b)  TK Culturally Sensitive (TK CS) — the Work contains content
          requiring special cultural protocols for access, use, and handling;
     (c)  TK Community Use Only (TK CO) — specified material is subject to
          community protocols restricting use by those outside the Community;
     (d)  TK Non-Commercial (TK NC) — the Work must not be used for
          commercial purposes without explicit prior written permission;
     (e)  TK Verified (TK V) — the Work has been created by a verified
          member of the specified Indigenous community;
     (f)  TK Multiple Communities (TK MC) — responsibilities for the Work are
          shared across the GTB and Beaver Island Band families, whether
          enrolled in GTB, LTBB, or elsewhere; and
     (g)  TK Family (TK F) — specified elements of the Work embody knowledge
          stewarded by particular Beaver Island Band families, whose
          authorization is required in addition to the Licensor's for any use
          of those elements.

4.3  LABELS AS CONDITIONS. Observance of the TK Labels, and of any updated
     Label text published by the Licensor on a Local Contexts Hub project
     page associated with the Work, is a condition of this License. Labels
     are metadata of governance, not merely informational tags.

4.4  INDIGENOUS DATA GOVERNANCE STANDARDS. All handling of the Work and of
     Derivative Data must comply with the CARE Principles for Indigenous Data
     Governance — Collective Benefit, Authority to Control, Responsibility,
     and Ethics (Carroll et al., Data Science Journal 19:43 (2020), DOI
     10.5334/dsj-2020-043) — taken together with, and never displaced by,
     FAIR data principles. Provenance of the Work and of Derivative Data
     must be documented consistent with IEEE 2890-2025 (IEEE Recommended
     Practice for Provenance of Indigenous Peoples' Data).

4.5  NO WAIVER OF INTERNATIONALLY RECOGNIZED RIGHTS. No rights are waived
     under UNDRIP, including without limitation: Article 11 (redress for
     cultural and intellectual property taken without free, prior and
     informed consent); Article 31 (the right to maintain, control, protect,
     and develop intellectual property over cultural heritage, Traditional
     Knowledge, and Cultural Expressions); Article 32 (free, prior and
     informed consent for projects affecting Indigenous resources); Article
     37 (treaty rights); and Article 40 (access to fair procedures and just
     remedies). Annex A states the legal status of each instrument; this
     License claims each instrument only to the extent of that status.

4.6  FAMILY-HELD KNOWLEDGE. Elements of the Work that derive from knowledge
     stewarded by specific Beaver Island Band families ("Family-held
     Knowledge") are subject to family-level protocols under TK F. No
     Licensee — expressly including any person or organization claiming
     Indigenous authority, affiliation, or adoption — may commercialize,
     teach, certify others in, publish curricular or spiritual material based
     on, or build derivative products from Family-held Knowledge, absent
     documented FPIC (Section 9) from the stewarding family or families as
     identified through the channels in Annex B. Misappropriated teachings,
     ceremonies, or knowledge-hybrids incorporating Family-held Knowledge do
     not become "free" by re-publication under another name or persona; the
     Licensor's and stewarding families' rights attach to the knowledge,
     not to any usurper's branding of it.

