5. INDIGENOUS DATA SOVEREIGNTY AND ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE RESTRICTIONS
============================================================================

5.1  NO AI TRAINING. As a condition of this License, the Work, Derivative
     Data, and outputs of the Work may NOT be used to train, fine-tune,
     align, evaluate, augment, or distill any Model; may not be incorporated
     into any training corpus, fine-tuning set, reinforcement-learning
     dataset, retrieval-augmented-generation index, or embedding store; and
     may not be supplied as input to AI systems for the purpose of producing
     a substitute or derivative of the Work. This prohibition is a license
     condition under Section 2.2, and its violation acts outside the scope of
     the license (Jacobsen v. Katzer, supra).

5.2  TEXT AND DATA MINING RESERVATION. All rights to perform text and data
     mining (TDM) of the Work, and all rights under any statutory TDM
     exception, are expressly RESERVED, including under Article 4(3) of
     Directive (EU) 2019/790 for Licensees subject to EU law. Machine-readable
     reservations (robots.txt, ai.txt, or equivalent declarations) accompany
     the Work and form part of this condition.

5.3  DERIVATIVE MODELS AND DATA ARE WITHIN THE LICENSE. Any Model, system
     output, or Derivative Data produced in violation of Section 5.1 is
     asserted by the Licensor to be an infringing derivative work or
     infringing copy to the maximum extent permitted by law, and remains
     irrevocably subject to this License, including Sections 3, 4, and 12.
     The Licensor's position follows the treatment of training-data
     conditions as scope-of-license limits and the rejection of blanket
     fair-use defenses where the use substitutes for the licensed work
     (cf. Thomson Reuters Enterprise Centre GmbH v. Ross Intelligence Inc.,
     No. 20-cv-613 (D. Del. Feb. 11, 2025) (non-generative AI; fair use
     rejected); appeal pending as of this License's issuance). The Licensor
     acknowledges that the law of generative-AI fair use remains unsettled
     as of 2026; this Section is drafted to protect the Licensor's position
     under either resolution, because it operates as a condition of the
     license and not solely as a prediction of fair-use outcomes.

5.4  PROVENANCE AND DISCLOSURE CONDITION. Any Licensee that applies for a
     patent or other registered right claiming an invention or design based
     on or derived from the Work or from Associated Traditional Knowledge
     must disclose the Work, the Licensor, and the Community of origin in the
     application. This condition voluntarily adopts the disclosure principle
     of Article 3 of the WIPO Treaty on Intellectual Property, Genetic
     Resources and Associated Traditional Knowledge (adopted May 24, 2024),
     as a matter of contract, regardless of United States non-party status.

5.5  NO SURVEILLANCE OR BIOMETRIC EXPLOITATION. The Work may not be used,
     directly or indirectly, for surveillance of members of the Community;
     for biometric or emotion-recognition processing of images or voices of
     Community members or of cultural performances; or for building identity
     systems profiling Indigenous persons or communities.

5.6  DATA DELETION AND ATTESTATION. Upon termination or revocation, every
     Licensee must delete all copies of the Work and Derivative Data
     (including from backups within 90 days), quarantine any Models trained
     in violation, and provide a written attestation of deletion signed by a
     responsible officer within 14 days of demand.

============================================================================
6. RESERVED SOVEREIGN RIGHTS AND TREATY FOUNDATIONS
============================================================================

6.1  RESERVED RIGHTS DOCTRINE. The Ottawa and Chippewa nations reserved all
     rights not expressly ceded in the Treaty of Washington (1836), 7 Stat.
     491, and the Treaty of Detroit (1855), 11 Stat. 621. Rights not
     expressly surrendered are retained: this is the reserved rights doctrine
     recognized in United States v. Winans, 198 U.S. 371 (1905), Winters v.
     United States, 207 U.S. 564 (1908), and reaffirmed in Minnesota v. Mille
     Lacs Band of Chippewa Indians, 526 U.S. 172 (1999) (treaty usufructuary
     rights survive statehood, removal pressure, and the passage of time
     absent express congressional abrogation). Treaty rights are federal law
     and prevail over conflicting state law under the Supremacy Clause (U.S.
     Const. art. VI, cl. 2; McGirt v. Oklahoma, 140 S. Ct. 2452 (2020)).

6.2  WHAT THE SUPREMACY CLAUSE DOES AND DOES NOT DO HERE. The Supremacy
     Clause makes the Treaties supreme over contrary state law; it does not,
     by itself, make a private license superior to other private agreements.
     This License therefore claims its coercive force from honest sources:
     (a)  federal copyright law and the condition doctrine (Section 2.2);
     (b)  the Licensee's consent, which also creates a Consensual
          Relationship supporting tribal regulatory and adjudicatory
          authority under Montana v. United States, 450 U.S. at 565;
     (c)  federal statutes protecting Indigenous cultural property
          (NAGPRA; the Indian Arts and Crafts Act); and
     (d)  the treaty-protected status of the underlying cultural,
          usufructuary, and heritage rights that the Work embodies and that
          this License exists to defend.
     Assertions of treaty supremacy in this License concern the underlying
     rights, not an independent claim that this contract outranks other
     contracts.

6.3  INDIAN CANONS OF CONSTRUCTION. To the maximum extent a tribunal
     permits, any ambiguity in this License concerning the scope of reserved
     treaty rights or the interpretation of the Treaties shall be construed
     in favor of the tribal interest, per the Indian canons of construction
     (Montana v. Blackfeet Tribe of Indians, 471 U.S. 759 (1985); Bryan v.
     Itasca County, 426 U.S. 373 (1976); County of Oneida v. Oneida Indian
     Nation, 470 U.S. 226 (1985)).

6.4  SURVIVING TREATY FISHERY JURISPRUDENCE. The continuing vitality of the
     1836 Treaty rights is affirmed by the Consent Decree entered August 24,
     2023, in United States v. Michigan, No. 2:73-cv-26 (W.D. Mich.) (Maloney,
     J.), governing treaty fishing in the waters ceded in 1836 through August
     2047; affirmed by the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit in
     March 2025; certiorari denied January 2026. The Grand Traverse Band is a
     party to that decree and a member of the Chippewa Ottawa Resource
     Authority (CORA). These instruments confirm — as current adjudicated
     fact, not mere aspiration — that the Treaty-Protected Interests are
     alive, federally enforced, and co-managed by the Licensor's Nation.

============================================================================
7. GREAT LAKES TREATY WATERS AND MARITIME DIMENSION
============================================================================

7.1  TREATY WATERS. The waters of Lakes Michigan, Huron, and Superior ceded
     in the Treaty of Washington (1836) are subject to retained tribal
     usufructuary and governance interests recognized in United States v.
     Michigan and its 1985, 2000, and 2023 Consent Decrees, implemented
     through CORA co-management. To the extent the Work embodies, describes,
     or depends upon fisheries, navigation, ecological, geographical, or
     place-name knowledge of the Beaver Island archipelago or other 1836
     ceded waters, that knowledge is Treaty-adjacent Traditional Knowledge
     within Section 4 and shall not be used in ways that materially harm the
     treaty-managed fishery, its habitats, or CORA co-management.

7.2  RESERVED SUBMERGED LANDS AND WATER RIGHTS. Nothing in this License
     cedes, diminishes, or waives (a) rights of tribes in the beds of
     navigable waters where those beds were reserved for a tribe (Montana v.
     United States, 450 U.S. 544 (1981) (recognizing the rule while applying
     its exception); Choctaw Nation v. Oklahoma, 397 U.S. 620 (1970); United
     States v. Holt State Bank, 270 U.S. 49 (1926)); or (b) federally
     reserved water rights as recognized in Winters v. United States, 207
     U.S. 564 (1908). These doctrines are invoked to show that Indigenous
     governance of water, including the water-column context of the Work and
     its data, follows the same reserved-rights logic as this License's
     governance of knowledge.

7.3  MARITIME AND ADMIRALTY PRESERVATION. Exclusive federal admiralty
     jurisdiction under 28 U.S.C. § 1333 cannot be created or removed by
     private license, and this License does neither. Instead: (a) disputes
     arising under this License that touch commercial activity on
     Treaty-ceded waters remain within the consensual jurisdiction framework
     of Section 14 as to the Licensee's obligations; (b) the Licensee is
     barred from using the Work aboard vessels or in maritime operations that
     materially interfere with exercise of Treaty-Protected Interests,
     including tribal commercial and subsistence fishing under the 2023
     Consent Decree; and (c) the Licensor reserves all rights and remedies
     available in admiralty, in federal question, or in tribal proceedings,
     without election.

7.4  CUSTOMARY INTERNATIONAL LAW OF THE SEA. Customary international law is
     part of United States law (The Paquete Habana, 175 U.S. 677, 700
     (1900)). The United States is not a party to the United Nations
     Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS), but accepts its navigational
     and resource-related provisions as reflective of customary international
     law (United States Oceans Policy Statement, 1983). This License invokes
     that customary framework honestly: as interpretive backdrop for the
     Community's enduring relationship to the Great Lakes waters, and as a
     rule of construction for cross-border Licensees — not as a claim that
     UNCLOS binds the United States as a treaty.

7.5  PRECEDENT OF MARINE TREATY RIGHTS. Courts have repeatedly enforced
     express treaty reservations of marine resource rights for Pacific
     Northwest tribes (e.g., the Treaty of Neah Bay (1855), 12 Stat. 939,
     reserving whaling and sealing rights; the Makah whaling litigation,
     Anderson v. Evans (9th Cir. 2004); the Boldt decision line under the
     Stevens treaties). The 1836 Treaty fishery now stands in the same modern
     enforcement posture through the 2023 Consent Decree. This Section
     records that lineage so that no reader mistakes the maritime dimension
     of this License for symbolism alone.

7.6  BOUNDARY WATERS. The Boundary Waters Treaty of 1909 (36 Stat. 2448)
     governs navigation and uses of US-Canada boundary waters and preserves
     pre-existing usage rights of the parties' nationals. The Licensee shall
     not use the Work to undermine Indigenous navigation, fishing, or
     stewardship rights in boundary and connecting waters of the Great Lakes.

============================================================================
8. CULTURAL PATRIMONY, BURIAL PROTECTIONS, AND ANTI-ALIENATION
============================================================================

8.1  NON-ALIENATION OF CULTURAL PATRIMONY. No Licensee shall use the Work,
     its data, or its outputs to facilitate, document for sale, market,
     broker, appraise for conveyance, or otherwise support the sale,
     transfer, or alienation of human remains, funerary objects, sacred
     objects, burial sites (including any portion of the Beaver Island
     archipelago burial landscape), or objects of Cultural Patrimony, each as
     defined in NAGPRA, 25 U.S.C. §§ 3001-3002. Under NAGPRA, Cultural
     Patrimony is inalienable by any individual; no transfer can extinguish
     the Community's interest.

8.2  RIGHT OF SEPULCHER AND NAGPRA PRINCIPLES. The Work shall not be used
     to disturb, commodify, decorate, redecorate, "restore," tour, or
     display the resting places of the ancestors or their associated
     structures, including spirit houses erected over burials. The
     common-law right of sepulcher and the principles of NAGPRA —
     including its recognition that burial and funerary materials carry
     continuing communal rights (25 U.S.C. §§ 3001-3013) — are conditions of
     any Licensee's use of the Work in any museum, educational, geospatial,
     real-estate, or heritage application.

8.3  DISCOVERY PROTOCOL. A Licensee who, in the course of using the Work,
     encounters or infers the apparent location of human remains, funerary
     objects, or undocumented sites of cultural significance within the
     Beaver Island archipelago or other Treaty-ceded territory must treat
     that information as restricted Derivative Data: no publication, no
     commercial use, prompt notice to the Licensor, and referral to the
     appropriate tribal authorities.

8.4  INDIAN ARTS AND CRAFTS ACT PRESERVATION. Where the Work or its
     Cultural Expressions are offered in commerce as authentic products of an
     Indigenous creator, the Indian Arts and Crafts Act applies by its own
     terms: civil actions authorize recovery of treble damages or statutory
     damages of not less than $1,000 for each day the violation continues,
     plus costs and attorney's fees (25 U.S.C. § 305e(b)); counterfeiting the
     Government trademark of the Indian Arts and Crafts Board is a federal
     crime (18 U.S.C. § 1158), as is misrepresentation of Indian-produced
     goods (18 U.S.C. § 1159). This License's remedies supplement, and never
     supplant, those statutory remedies.

============================================================================
9. PRIOR INFORMED CONSENT
============================================================================

9.1  REQUIREMENT. Any use beyond the Permissible Uses of Section 11 requires
     free, prior, and informed consent ("FPIC") from the Licensor, on the
     model of UNDRIP Articles 19 and 32. FPIC must be:
     (a)  obtained in writing before the use begins;
     (b)  based on full disclosure of purposes, methods, retention, and
          outcomes, including any intended AI-related processing, which is
          presumptively refused;
     (c)  revocable at any time under Section 12;
     (d)  non-transferable to any third party without explicit permission;
          and
     (e)  contingent upon periodic reporting of usage and outcomes.

9.2  COMMUNITY CONSULTATION FOR SENSITIVE MATERIAL. Where a proposed use
     reaches TK CS (culturally sensitive), TK SS (secret/sacred), TK CL
     (clan), or TK SS-adjacent material, the Licensor will, before granting
     consent, consult within the Community consistent with Community
     Protocols. Silence is not consent.

============================================================================
10. PROHIBITED USES
============================================================================

The Work may not be used by any Licensee for:

     (a)  training, fine-tuning, evaluating, or building Models (Section 5);
     (b)  commercial applications without explicit written permission (TK NC);
     (c)  any use that misappropriates, misrepresents, or distorts Indigenous
          cultural expression;
     (d)  any use violating the Licensor's moral rights of attribution and
          integrity, including under 17 U.S.C. § 106A where applicable and
          under contract for all components of the Work where § 106A does not
          reach;
     (e)  any application that harms or exploits Indigenous communities or
          knowledge systems;
     (f)  military applications or weapons development of any kind, including
          naval weapons, maritime-domain surveillance systems, autonomous
          vessel targeting, and battlefield command systems;
     (g)  any use promoting cultural appropriation or stereotyping;
     (h)  data mining, scraping, or extraction without FPIC (Sections 5, 9);
     (i)  any use contrary to Community Protocols;
     (j)  any use that fails to respect the spiritual or cultural significance
          of embedded knowledge;
     (k)  any use facilitating the sale, transfer, or alienation of Cultural
          Patrimony, burial sites, human remains, or funerary and sacred
          objects (Section 8);
     (l)  surveillance, biometric exploitation, or identity profiling of
          Community members (Section 5.5);
     (m) use in maritime operations materially interfering with
          Treaty-Protected Interests (Section 7.3(b)); and
     (n)  removal, alteration, or falsification of CMI (Section 3.3).

============================================================================
11. PERMISSIBLE USES AND COMMUNITY BENEFIT
============================================================================

11.1 PERMISSIBLE USES. Subject to all conditions:
     (a)  educational use with proper attribution and preserved cultural
          context;
     (b)  research use, provided findings are shared with the Licensor;
     (c)  non-commercial Community development with prior notification;
     (d)  uses that promote accurate cultural understanding and
          representation; and
     (e)  uses employing culturally appropriate methods of engagement with
          the Work.

11.2 COMMUNITY BENEFIT LICENSEES. Members of the Community (Section 1.8) —
     expressly including Beaver Island Band families and descendants
     regardless of GTB or LTBB enrollment — receive a standing, no-cost
     license for non-commercial cultural, educational, language-
     revitalization, resource-stewardship, and community-development uses,
     conditioned only on observance of Community Protocols and Sections 3
     (attribution) and 8 (patrimony protections). No Consent under Section 9
     is needed for Section 11.2 uses. This Section, with Section 1.8, is the
     mechanism by which this License delivers collective benefit as required
     by the CARE Principles.

============================================================================
12. SOVEREIGN REVOCATION, TERMINATION, AND COMPLIANCE DUTIES
============================================================================

12.1 REVOCATION RIGHT. The Licensor reserves the express contractual and
     license-conditioned right to revoke any Licensee's rights, with respect
     to any Work embodying Traditional Knowledge or Cultural Expression, (a)
     for material breach of any condition — automatically and immediately
     (Section 2.2); and (b) as to TK-bearing components, upon 30 days'
     written notice for any reason grounded in protection of the Community's
     cultural integrity, exercise of Community Protocols, or defense of
     Treaty-Protected Interests.

12.2 FALLBACK LADDER. If a tribunal holds clause 12.1(b) unenforceable in
     whole or part, the following remain fully in force, in descending order:
     (i)  automatic termination for material breach (12.1(a));
     (ii) revocation upon 90 days' notice for breach-of-protocol cause;
     (iii) the non-renewable fixed term implied from the grant, with all
          conditions surviving; and
     (iv) injunctive relief under copyright law for continued use outside
          scope (17 U.S.C. § 502).

12.3 COMPLIANCE UPON REVOCATION. Upon notice of revocation, the Licensee
     must: cease all use; remove and delete all copies, Derivative Data, and
     quarantined Models (Section 5.6); provide written officer-signed
     confirmation within 14 days; and, upon request, deliver up or attest to
     destruction of derivative materials embodying the Work (consistent with
     17 U.S.C. § 503 disposition remedies).

12.4 NO PURPORTED CRIMINAL SANCTIONS. This License creates civil rights
     only. Criminal provisions cited herein (e.g., 18 U.S.C. §§ 1158-1159)
     are federal statutes enforceable by federal authorities, not by the
     Licensor and not by any tribal forum; tribal criminal jurisdiction over
     nonmembers is foreclosed by federal law (Oliphant v. Suquamish Indian
     Tribe, 435 U.S. 191 (1978), and its narrow statutory exceptions, none of
     which concern licensing).

============================================================================
13. COMPENSATION, DAMAGES, AND REMEDIES
============================================================================

13.1 ELECTION OF REMEDIES. For infringement or condition-breach, the
     Licensor may elect, as authorized by law: (a) actual damages plus the
     infringer's profits attributable to the infringement (17 U.S.C.
     § 504(b)); or (b) statutory damages under 17 U.S.C. § 504(c), which for
     willful infringement permit awards up to $150,000 per work infringed —
     the congressional benchmark this License adopts as its reference
     quantum; plus (c) full costs and attorney's fees (17 U.S.C. § 505); and
     (d) for CMI violations, the separate remedies of 17 U.S.C. § 1203.

13.2 CONTRACTUAL DAMAGES — PRIMARY ASSERTION. As bargained contractual
     damages in addition to copyright remedies, the Licensor asserts: a
     royalty of not less than 20% of gross revenue derived from unauthorized
     use; liquidated damages of $10,000 per day of unauthorized use following
     notice; and disgorgement of non-monetary benefit at fair market value.
     The parties stipulate these figures are a reasonable pre-estimate of
     harm that is genuinely difficult to quantify, including cultural and
     spiritual harm not fully compensable in money.

13.3 FALLBACK LADDER (DAMAGES). To the extent any amount in Section 13.2
     is held an unenforceable penalty or otherwise reduced, the claim
     automatically descends to the highest enforceable rung, without
     interruption: (i) liquidated damages as judicially reduced or
     blue-penciled; (ii) statutory damages under § 504(c), with willfulness
     enhancement; (iii) the IACA benchmark of not less than $1,000 per day of
     continuing violation where misrepresentation as Indigenous-produced
     material is implicated (25 U.S.C. § 305e(b)); (iv) actual damages and
     § 504(b) profits; and (v) restitution of the reasonable value of the
     benefit conferred. No rung's failure affects the rungs below it.

13.4 ADDITIONAL RECOVERABLE ITEMS. The Licensee is liable for: all legal
     and professional fees incurred in pursuing recovery; costs of cultural
     restoration where applicable; costs of public correction of
     misrepresentations; and reasonable administrative costs of compliance
     verification.

13.5 DELIVER-UP, NOT SELF-HELP SEIZURE. The Licensor's remedies of
     disposition are limited to court-ordered impoundment, forfeiture,
     destruction, or deliver-up of infringing articles (17 U.S.C. § 503), and
     to assignment of rights in derivative works where a tribunal so orders.
     This License does not authorize self-help seizure of a Licensee's
     equipment, domains, or accounts.

13.6 NO ADVANCE WAIVER OF LIMITATIONS. Copyright claims are subject to the
     three-year limitations period of 17 U.S.C. § 507(b), measured from when
     each claim accrues; the Licensee stipulates that each day of a
     continuing violation accrues anew. Contractual rights of revocation and
     injunctive relief for cultural-protocol conditions are continuing and
     not waived by delay; any purported advance waiver of limitations
     defenses is excised under Section 17 rather than applied to bar
     meritorious defenses of law.

============================================================================
14. DISPUTE RESOLUTION: TRIBAL COURTS, EXHAUSTION, COMITY, ARBITRATION
============================================================================

14.1 CONSENSUAL-RELATIONSHIP JURISDICTION. By accepting this License, every
     Licensee enters a Consensual Relationship with a tribal member through
     contract (Section 1.10), expressly consents to the civil jurisdiction of
     the Grand Traverse Band Tribal Court for disputes under this License,
     and waives objections to that court's personal jurisdiction over it for
     such disputes. The Licensor asserts — and the Licensee does not contest
     — that a licensing contract is a relationship "through commercial
     dealing, contracts, leases, or other arrangements" under Montana v.
     United States, 450 U.S. at 565, and that this nexus supports GTB civil
     jurisdiction over the Licensee's compliance obligations.

14.2 PRIMARY FORUM; TRIBAL EXHAUSTION. The Grand Traverse Band Tribal Court
     is the primary forum for disputes concerning Community Protocols and the
     cultural conditions of this License. Parties subject themselves to the
     tribal exhaustion doctrine: tribal jurisdiction questions are decided
     first by the tribal court itself (National Farmers Union Ins. Cos. v.
     Crow Tribe, 471 U.S. 845 (1985); Iowa Mutual Ins. Co. v. LaPlante, 480
     U.S. 9 (1987)). Copyright claims remain subject to exclusive federal
     jurisdiction under 28 U.S.C. § 1338; the Licensee consents that federal
     enforcement follow, rather than precede, the tribal court's rulings on
     cultural and contractual conditions, and the Licensor may elect either
     sequence.

14.3 ALTERNATIVE TRIBAL FORUM. If the GTB Tribal Court declines, is unable,
     or lacks subject-matter jurisdiction to proceed, the Licensor may
     designate another federally recognized tribal court or traditional
     dispute-resolution forum willing to accept the matter, with preference
     for forums connected to the Ottawa and Chippewa Nations. The Licensee's
     consent in Section 14.1 extends to such designated forum. This License
     does not claim that tribal adjudicatory power over nonmembers is
     unlimited; it rests on consent, on the Montana first exception, and on
     the designated forum's own law.

14.4 RECOGNITION OF TRIBAL JUDGMENTS — THE CORRECT MECHANISM. The Licensee
     acknowledges: (a) 28 U.S.C. § 1738 does not by its terms govern tribal
     judgments, and federal recognition of tribal court judgments rests on
     comity, governed by Wilson v. Marchington, 127 F.3d 805 (9th Cir. 1997)
     (recognition where the tribal court had jurisdiction, the proceeding was
     conducted with due process, and the judgment does not affront the
     recognizing court's public policy); (b) the Licensee stipulates, for
     comity purposes and to the extent a court accepts stipulations of fact,
     that the forums designated under this Section provide impartial tribunals
     and due process; (c) in Michigan, recognition and enforcement of tribal
     court judgments is governed by Michigan Court Rule 2.615; and (d)
     statutory full-faith-and-credit obligations that do reach tribal orders
     (e.g., 25 U.S.C. § 1911(d); 28 U.S.C. § 1738B; 18 U.S.C. § 2265) remain
     unaffected by this License.

14.5 WAIVER OF FORUM OBJECTIONS. The Licensee waives forum non conveniens
     and similar discretionary objections to the forums designated under this
     Section, to the extent such waivers are enforceable, consistent with the
     policy favoring enforcement of forum-selection clauses.

14.6 ARBITRATION LADDER (PRAGMATIC ENFORCEMENT TRACK). At the Licensor's
     election, disputes may finally be resolved by arbitration under the
     Federal Arbitration Act, 9 U.S.C. §§ 1-16, before arbitrators with
     demonstrated federal Indian law expertise. An arbitral award is
     confirmable in federal court under 9 U.S.C. § 9. The inclusion of this
     track follows the structure by which Indian law questions are decided
     alongside arbitral selection (cf. C&L Enterprises v. Citizen Band
     Potawatomi Indian Tribe, 532 U.S. 411 (2001) (forum and waiver terms
     construed strictly). This track exists so that an enforceable federal
     judgment is always reachable even where comity recognition is uncertain.

14.7 FEDERAL COURT ACCESS PRESERVED. Nothing in this Section divests the
     federal courts of jurisdiction the Licensor may invoke under 28 U.S.C.
     §§ 1331, 1338, or 1362 (the latter running to tribes as plaintiffs); nor
     does it waive the Licensor's right to seek temporary or preliminary
     injunctive relief in any court of competent jurisdiction to preserve the
     status quo pending exhaustion or arbitration.

